    "exam_type": "JEE",
    "question_type": "single",
    "question_text": "The energy levels of an atom is shown is figure.\n\nEnergy levels:\n$1: 0.0\\text{ eV}$\n$2: -2.2\\text{ eV}$\n$3: -5.2\\text{ eV}$\n$4: -10.0\\text{ eV}$\n\nTransitions:\n$\\text{A: } 1 \\rightarrow 2$\n$\\text{B: } 1 \\rightarrow 3$\n$\\text{C: } 2 \\rightarrow 3$\n$\\text{D: } 1 \\rightarrow 4$\n\nWhich one of these transitions will result in the emission of a photon of wavelength $124.1\\text{ nm}$? Given ($h = 6.62 \\times 10^{-34}\\text{ J s}$)",
    "options": [
      "B",
      "A",
      "C",
      "D"
    ],
    "correct_index": 3,
    "correct_indices": [
      3
    ],
    "numeric_answer": null,
    "numerical_tolerance": 0,
    "marks": 4,
    "negative_marks": 1,
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "status": "available",
    "solution": "The change in energy is related to wavelength of photon emitted as:\n$$\\Delta E = \\frac{hc}{\\lambda} = \\frac{6.62 \\times 10^{-34}\\text{ J s} \\times 3 \\times 10^8\\text{ ms}^{-1}}{1.6 \\times 10^{-19}\\text{ J} \\times 124.1\\text{ nm}} = \\frac{1242\\text{ eV nm}}{124.1\\text{ nm}} = 10\\text{ eV}$$\nHence, option D is correct.",
