    "topic": "Atomic",
    "exam_type": "JEE",
    "question_type": "numerical",
    "question_text": "Using Bohr's model, calculate the ratio of the magnetic fields generated due to the motion of the electrons in the $2^{\\text{nd}}$ and $4^{\\text{th}}$ orbits of hydrogen atom ______.",
    "options": [],
    "correct_index": null,
    "correct_indices": [],
    "numeric_answer": 32,
    "numerical_tolerance": 0.05,
    "marks": 4,
    "negative_marks": 0,
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "status": "available",
    "solution": "The magnetic field at the center of a circular orbit is given by $B = \\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2r}$.\n\nThe equivalent current due to the revolving electron is $I = \\frac{e}{T} = \\frac{ev}{2\\pi r}$.\n\nSubstituting $I$ into the expression for $B$, we get $B = \\frac{\\mu_0 ev}{4\\pi r^2}$.\n\nAccording to Bohr's model for a hydrogen atom, the velocity of the electron in the $n^{\\text{th}}$ orbit is $v \\propto \\frac{1}{n}$ and the radius of the orbit is $r \\propto n^2$.\n\nTherefore, the magnetic field $B$ depends on the principal quantum number $n$ as $B \\propto \\frac{1/n}{(n^2)^2} \\Rightarrow B \\propto \\frac{1}{n^5}$.\n\nThe ratio of the magnetic fields in the $2^{\\text{nd}}$ and $4^{\\text{th}}$ orbits is $\\frac{B_2}{B_4} = \\left(\\frac{n_4}{n_2}\\right)^5 = \\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right)^5 = 2^5 = 32$.",
